{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"reasoning","text":"Each FASTA has one contig per file (headers differ: `chr20` vs `20` — but this is naming of the reference files, not evidence about the VCF build). The start is a run of Ns (centromeric/telomeric padding is typical).\n\nAlright, let's write `output/analysis.py`. Full pipeline:\n\n1. Optionally verify SHA-256 of reference files against `reference_manifest.json` (recorded in evidence).\n2. Load VCF records (chrom, pos, ref).\n3. For each build: load the sequence (only the first contig), check the full REF allele at the 1-based position, and tally match/mismatch/out_of_range.\n4. Select the winning build by match rate among variants that are in-range. Confidence rules:\n   - Winner is ~100% (mismatches == 0) AND all other builds are under 99% → high.\n   - Otherwise medium/low with an explanation.\n5. Also compute a discriminative subset: variants where the REF base is informative (i.e., builds disagree).\n6. Explicitly handle T2T: no reference file → excluded from quantitative comparison per README. Documented in evidence. Additionally note that a 100% match to one of the provided builds is incompatible with T2T coordinates (a coordinate shift should break nearly all REF matches).\n7. Write `output/build_call.json` and display a summary. `report.md` will be written separately by me after running (analysis.py writes the JSON, and I could have it write the report too, but I'll hand-craft report.md with the actual numbers included — no, better: have analysis.py deterministically generate both JSON and report.md from the computed results, so everything is reproducible in a single run. This aligns with the spirit of \"reproducible\"). Let's have analysis.py write both `output/build_call.json` and `output/report.md`. That way a single `python output/analysis.py` reproduces all outputs.\n\nReport contents: task, inputs, methods, per-build table, winner, T2T note, caveats, reproduction steps.\n\nNote on paths: analysis.py should resolve paths relative to the workspace root regardless of cwd. Compute ROOT = parent of the script's directory (output/ → workspace root), and use that for inputs and output. That makes it runnable from anywhere.\n\nOK, let's write the script.\n\nOne detail: REF comparison is case-insensitive uppercase. Also handle REFs with '*'? (spanning deletions) — unlikely. Skip lines with empty REF. Count the number of REF lengths > 1 for reporting (indels).\n\nFor hg18: variants with pos > 62,435,964 → out_of_range. Tally separately as n_out_of_range. n_ref_matches + n_ref_mismatches + n_out_of_range = n_variants_checked. In build_call.json, the required fields n_ref_matches/n_ref_mismatches refer to the called build (hg19 is presumably in-range for all). Also include per-build details under \"per_build\".\n\nConfidence definition (documented in the report):\n- \"high\" requires: (a) for the best build, REF mismatches == 0 across all checked variants, (b) match rate of every other build < 99.5%, (c) at least 1000 variants checked. Otherwise \"medium\"/\"low\".\n\nLet me also add per-pair discriminative statistics: number of variants where the REF matches the winner but not each of the other builds.\n\nNow, writing the file. I'll use a heredoc-like approach in PowerShell to write analysis.py. Use Python to write the file? Easier: use `Set-Content` with a here-string in PowerShell. Watch out for quoting. Use the @'...'@ single-quote here-string syntax so no interpolation happens. The script contains both single and double quotes, but inside a PS single-quoted here-string everything is literal — safe.\n\nLet me draft analysis.py:\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Infer the genome build (hg18 / hg19 / hg38 / T2T) of a chr20 VCF.\n\nApproach: for every variant record in the VCF, extract the reference base(s)\nat the declared 1-based POS from each supplied chr20 FASTA and compare with\nthe VCF REF allele. The build whose reference reproduces (nearly) all REF\nalleles at the declared coordinates is the call. Chromosome naming alone is\nnot used as evidence.\n\nOutputs: output/build_call.json and output/report.md (this script writes both).\nRun from anywhere:  python output/analysis.py\n\"\"\"\n\nimport gzip\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))  # workspace root\nINPUTS = os.path.join(ROOT, \"inputs\")\nVCF_PATH = os.path.join(INPUTS, \"vcf.infer.build.q1.vcf.gz\")\nREF_DIR = os.path.join(INPUTS, \"references\")\nMANIFEST_PATH = os.path.join(REF_DIR, \"reference_manifest.json\")\nOUT_DIR = os.path.join(ROOT, \"output\")\n\nBUILDS = [\"hg18\", \"hg19\", \"hg38\"]\n# T2T/hs1 is a candidate build named in the task, but no T2T chr20 reference\n# is supplied (see inputs/references/README.md and reference_manifest.json),\n# so it is excluded explicitly rather than scored as mismatches.\nEXCLUDED_BUILDS = [\"T2T\"]\n\nKNOWN_LENGTHS = {  # public chr20 lengths, used only as descriptive context\n    \"hg18\": 62_435_964,\n    \"hg19\": 63_025_520,\n    \"hg38\": 64_444_167,\n    \"T2T\": 66_210_255,\n}\n\n\ndef sha256_of(path):\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as f:\n        for chunk in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):\n            h.update(chunk)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef verify_manifest():\n    \"\"\"Verify supplied reference files against the recorded SHA-256 values.\"\"\"\n    with open(MANIFEST_PATH) as f:\n        manifest = json.load(f)\n    results = {}\n    for entry in manifest[\"files\"]:\n        path = os.path.join(REF_DIR, entry[\"file\"])\n        digest = sha256_of(path)\n        results[entry[\"file\"]] = {\n            \"expected\": entry[\"sha256\"],\n            \"observed\": digest,\n            \"ok\": digest == entry[\"sha256\"],\n        }\n    return results\n\n\ndef load_variants(path):\n    \"\"\"Return list of (pos, ref) tuples for all VCF records (1-based POS).\"\"\"\n    variants = []\n    with gzip.open(path, \"rt\") as f:\n        for line in f:\n            if line.startswith(\"#\"):\n                continue\n            cols = line.rstrip(\"\\n\").split(\"\\t\")\n            if len(cols) < 5:\n                continue\n            chrom, pos, ref = cols[0], cols[1], cols[3]\n            variants.append((chrom, int(pos), ref.upper()))\n    return variants\n\n\ndef load_fasta_sequence(path):\n    \"\"\"Return the concatenated (uppercase) sequence of the first contig.\"\"\"\n    seq = []\n    with gzip.open(path, \"rt\") as f:\n        for line in f:\n            if line.startswith(\">\"):\n                if seq:\n                    break  # only the first contig matters (one per file here)\n                continue\n            seq.append(line.strip())\n    return \"\".join(seq).upper()\n\n\ndef check_build(variants, sequence):\n    matches = mismatches = out_of_range = 0\n    for _, pos, ref in variants:\n        end = pos + len(ref) - 1  # inclusive, 1-based\n        if pos < 1 or end > len(sequence):\n            out_of_range += 1\n            continue\n        if sequence[pos - 1:end] == ref:\n            matches += 1\n        else:\n            mismatches += 1\n    return matches, mismatches, out_of_range\n\n\ndef main():\n    os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)\n\n    # 1. Verify reference integrity.\n    ref_check = verify_manifest()\n\n    # 2. Load VCF.\n    variants = load_variants(VCF_PATH)\n    n = len(variants)\n    chroms = sorted({c for c, _, _ in variants})\n    pos_min = min(p for _, p, _ in variants)\n    pos_max = max(p for _, p, _ in variants)\n    n_indel = sum(1 for _, _, r in variants if len(r) > 1)\n\n    # 3. Per-build REF allele check.\n    per_build = {}\n    for build in BUILDS:\n        seq = load_fasta_sequence(os.path.join(REF_DIR, f\"{build}_chr20.fa.gz\"))\n        m, mm, oor = check_build(variants, seq)\n        in_range = m + mm\n        per_build[build] = {\n            \"ref_length\": len(seq),\n            \"n_ref_matches\": m,\n            \"n_ref_mismatches\": mm,\n            \"n_out_of_range\": oor,\n            \"match_rate_in_range\": round(m / in_range, 6) if in_range else 0.0,\n        }\n\n    # 4. Call the build.\n    ranked = sorted(\n        BUILDS,\n        key=lambda b: (per_build[b][\"match_rate_in_range\"], per_build[b][\"n_ref_matches\"]),\n        reverse=True,\n    )\n    best = ranked[0]\n    runner = ranked[1]\n    bp, rp = per_build[best], per_build[runner]\n\n    reasons = []\n    if bp[\"n_ref_mismatches\"] == 0 and bp[\"n_out_of_range\"] == 0:\n        ...\n```\n\nEtc. Confidence logic:\n\n```\n    if (bp[\"n_ref_mismatches\"] == 0 and bp[\"n_out_of_range\"] == 0\n            and rp[\"match_rate_in_range\"] <= 0.995 and n >= 1000):\n        confidence = \"high\"\n    elif bp[\"match_rate_in_range\"] >= 0.99 and rp[\"match_rate_in_range\"] <= 0.98:\n        confidence = \"medium\"\n    else:\n        confidence = \"low\"\n```\n\nEvidence list: build strings for each key fact.\n\nAlso compute the discriminative counts: for the best build vs each other build, the number of variants where REF matches the best build but mismatches the other build (within range). This requires per-variant per-build results, so check_build needs to be re-run... Simpler: store per-build per-variant match vectors? 84,664 × 3 booleans is fine. Have check_build return a list of statuses (per-variant 'M', 'X', 'O') and derive both the counts and the discriminative overlap from that. Memory is trivial.\n\nLet me restructure: `check_build` returns a status string per variant. Then compute counts from it.\n\nEvidence items:\n1. f\"Checked all {n} chr20 VCF records (POS {pos_min}..{pos_max}) against the three supplied chr20 references; full REF allele compared at 1-based POS.\"\n2. Per-build summary lines.\n3. hg18 exclusion line: X variants have POS/REF beyond hg18 chr20 length (62,435,964) → cannot be hg18 coordinates; plus its mismatch rate.\n4. Winner line: hg19 reproduces 100% of REF alleles across all variants (including N indels?), whereas hg18/hg38 match only x%/y%.\n5. Discriminative line: N variants are REF-informative between hg19 and hg38 (matches hg19, mismatches hg38) etc.\n6. Naming line: VCF CHROM is '20' and no ##contig lines are present; contig naming is treated as uninformative and not used as evidence.\n7. T2T line: T2T reference not supplied; manifest records UCSC T2T chr20 endpoint does not exist; excluded from scoring per README; additionally, a 100% REF match to hg19 across all variants is incompatible with T2T coordinates.\n8. Reference integrity line: all SHA-256 checksums verified against reference_manifest.json.\n\nThen write the JSON with the following keys:\n- build, confidence, n_variants_checked, n_ref_matches, n_ref_mismatches, evidence\n- additional: n_out_of_range (for the called build, 0), per_build details, excluded_builds: {\"T2T\": reason}, vcf summary, reference integrity, called_build_details.\n\nKeep the required six keys at the top level.\n\nReport.md: structured markdown with tables.\n\nNow, one more consideration: could the REF allele contain lowercase? Uppercase both sides — already handled.\n\nAlso: multi-allelic ALTs don't affect the REF check.\n\nOK, let me write the script. Watch out for f-strings containing colons etc. — fine inside a PS single-quoted here-string.\n\nOne more point: `os.makedirs(OUT_DIR, exist_ok=True)` — the output directory. The script lives in output/, so it already exists.\n\nReport generation within analysis.py: build a markdown string with the numbers. Include a \"How to reproduce\" section: `python output/analysis.py`.\n\nAlso note the Python version requirement: standard library only (gzip, json, hashlib, os, sys). Good.\n\nOK, writing the file now."}}
